83.57,18.7],[83.67,18.61],[83.91,18.61],[84.1,18.67],[84.3,18.91],[84.49,19.02],[84.62,19.15],[84.64,19.28],[84.49,19.41],[84.49,19.92]]],[[[76.39,9.77],[76.36,9.79],[76.34,9.77],[76.34,9.53],[76.44,9.62],[76.39,9.77]]]]},"properties":{"class_key":"coastal_plains","name":"Coastal Plains","color":"#00cdae","sub_divisions":"Western Coastal Plains (Kachchh/Kathiawar, Konkan, Kannad, and Malabar Coast) and Eastern Coastal Plains (Northern Circars and Coromandel Coast).","geological_origin":"Formed along the fault-scarped trailing margins of the Peninsular landmass through a combination of marine transgressions/regressions, coastal wave erosion, and intense fluvial alluvium deposition by deltaic rivers.","relief_and_topography":"Contrasted morphologies: The West Coast is narrow (10–50 km), submerged/indented, and flanked by steep scarps with coastal lagoons (*kayals*); the East Coast is broad (80–130 km), emergent, and composed of expansive river deltas.","drainage_and_rivers":"Western drainage: Short, high-velocity, torrential rivers lacking deltas (Periyar, Bharathappuzha, Mandovi, Zuari, Sharavathi) forming estuaries. Eastern drainage: Major delta-building trunk streams (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri).","distinctive_features":"Presence of major coastal lagoons and wetlands (Vembanad and Ashtamudi in Malabar; Chilika, Kolleru, and Pulicat on the East Coast); significant contrast between natural deep-water ports on the west and shallow deltaic shores on the east.","economic_significance":"Gateway to India's international seaborne commerce with major deep-water ports (Kandla, Mumbai/JNPT, Mormugao, Kochi, Chennai, Visakhapatnam); massive offshore petroleum basins (Mumbai High, KG Basin); marine fishing and aquaculture; high-yield paddy and coconut agro-economies.","states_covered":["Gujarat","Maharashtra","Goa","Karnataka","Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh","Odisha","West Bengal","Puducherry"]}}
  ]
